  // ===== 18 Discounted Dividend Valuation: Mock 5 Q23-24 (two-stage DDM with a P/E terminal value), numbers changed =====
  setDay("2026-11-07");
  {
  const dd = "<details class=\"kq-vig\" open><summary>Vignette: Radiant Energy (two-stage DDM)</summary><div class=\"kq-vigbody\"><p>Brian Dawes, an analyst at a UK-based globally diversified equity mutual fund, has been assigned the task of estimating a fair value of the common stock of Radiant Energy. Dawes is aware of several approaches that could be used for this purpose. After carefully considering the characteristics of the company and its competitors, he believes Radiant will have extraordinary growth for the next few years and normal growth thereafter. So, he has concluded that a two-stage DDM is the most appropriate for valuing the stock.</p><p>Radiant pays semi-annual dividends. The total dividends during 2016, 2017, and 2018 have been C$0.126, C$0.160, and C$0.184, respectively. These imply a growth rate of 27% in 2017 and 15% in 2018. Dawes believes that the growth rate will be 12% in the next year. He has estimated that the first stage will include the next six years.</p><table class=\"kq-table\"><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Total dividends (C$)</th><th>Growth</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>2016</th><td>0.126</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><th>2017</th><td>0.160</td><td>27%</td></tr><tr><th>2018</th><td>0.184</td><td>15%</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Dawes is using the CAPM to estimate the required return on equity for Radiant. He has estimated that the company's beta, as measured against the S&amp;P/TSX Composite Index (formerly TSE 300 Composite Index), is 0.92. The Canadian risk-free rate, as measured by the annual yield on the 10-year government bond, is 3.8%. The equity risk premium for the Canadian market is estimated at 5.2%. Based on these data, Dawes has estimated that the required return on Radiant Energy's stock is 0.038 + 0.92(0.052) = 0.08584, or 8.584%. Dawes is doing the analysis in January 2019, and the stock price at that time is C$11.40.</p><p>Dawes realizes that even within the two-stage DDM, there could be some variations in the approach. He would like to explore how these variations affect the stock's valuation. Specifically, he wants to estimate the value of the stock for each of the following approaches separately.</p><p><b>Approach 1</b></p><p>The dividend growth rate will be 12% throughout the first stage of six years. The dividend growth rate thereafter will be 6%.</p><p><b>Approach 2</b></p><p>Instead of using the estimated stable growth rate of 6% in the second stage, Dawes wants to use his estimate that six years later, Radiant Energy's stock will be worth 15 times its earnings per share (trailing P/E of 15). He expects that the earnings retention ratio at that time will be 0.65.</p><p><b>Approach 3</b></p><p>In contrast to the first approach, in which the growth rate declines abruptly from 12% in the sixth year to 6% in the seventh, the growth rate would decline linearly from 12% in the first year to 6% in the seventh.</p></div></details>";
  mc(18, "What is the stock's terminal value (at the end of the first stage) based on Approach 2 (earnings multiple)?", ["C$15.57", "C$8.38", "C$5.45"], 0, "Approach 2: V6 = (trailing P/E) × E6 and E6 = D6 ÷ payout ratio. D6 = 0.184 × 1.12⁶ = C$0.3632. Payout = 1 − 0.65 = 0.35, so E6 = 0.3632 ÷ 0.35 = C$1.0377. V6 = 15 × 1.0377 = C$15.57. Slips: C$8.38 divides by the retention ratio (0.65) instead of the payout ratio; C$5.45 multiplies the P/E by the dividend instead of the earnings.", dd);
  mc(18, "What is the stock's current value based on Approach 2?", ["C$10.73", "C$9.50", "C$16.80"], 0, "V0 = PV of the dividends D1 to D6 + PV of V6. Required return r = 0.038 + 0.92(0.052) = 8.584%. PV of V6 = 15.5650 ÷ 1.08584⁶ = C$9.4963. PV of D1 to D6 = C$1.2321 (D1 = 0.2061, then each year ×1.12). V0 = 9.4963 + 1.2321 = C$10.73. Slips: C$9.50 leaves out the PV of the dividends; C$16.80 does not discount the terminal value.", dd);
  }
